From 0304fdb23195435372213fd9fdf15187362bcc23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 18:30:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 04/15] playback: GStreamer QoS handling, YUV pack path and a
 CJK fallback font

---
 README.md                    |  27 +++++--
 src/app/App.cpp              |  78 +++++++++++++++++++++
 src/core/GStreamerPlayer.cpp | 132 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 src/render/Gl2Context.cpp    |  33 ++++++---
 src/render/Gl2Context.h      |   5 +-
 src/render/Gl2Texture.cpp    |  28 ++++++--
 src/render/RenderTexture.h   |  25 +++++--
 7 files changed, 293 insertions(+), 35 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 02318ee..ad0a826 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -197,9 +197,10 @@ Rendering goes through the small `RenderBackend` interface
   and thumbnails are padded to power-of-two textures; frames larger than
   `GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE` are scaled down inside the GStreamer pipeline. Where
   the driver has `GL_APPLE_ycbcr_422` (every Mac GPU, QuickTime's own path)
-  frames are requested as 4:2:2 `UYVY` and uploaded as is: the GPU does the
-  colour conversion while sampling, the CPU only packs chroma instead of
-  running a YUV-to-RGB pass, and each frame is half the bytes. Elsewhere
+  the decoder's native I420 is packed to 4:2:2 `UYVY` by ImTube itself in the
+  copy it makes anyway, so `videoconvert` is passthrough, and the frame is
+  uploaded as is: the GPU does the colour conversion while sampling and each
+  frame is half the bytes of a 32-bit layout. Elsewhere
   frames come in the packed `GL_BGRA` + `GL_UNSIGNED_INT_8_8_8_8_REV` layout
   Apple's drivers upload without a swizzle pass (`ARGB` bytes on big-endian,
   `BGRA` on little-endian). The `[gl2]` startup line shows which is in use.
@@ -285,9 +286,23 @@ greyed out when none is installed; the decoders decodebin actually picks are
 printed at playback start as `[gst] decoder: ...`. `IMTUBE_GL_FORCE_POT=1` in the
 environment forces the power-of-two texture path on a GPU that does not need
 it, for testing. `IMTUBE_GL_YCBCR=0` disables the 4:2:2 video upload path
-(32-bit frames instead, for A/B comparisons); `IMTUBE_GL_YCBCR=swap` flips
-the 16-bit upload type between `8_8` and `8_8_REV`, the diagnostic to reach
-for if a driver ever shows the picture with luma and chroma exchanged.
+(32-bit frames instead, for A/B comparisons); `IMTUBE_GL_YCBCR=yuy2` requests
+the byte-swapped `YUY2` layout instead of `UYVY`, which takes a different
+GStreamer conversion routine and the other upload type, so a fault in either
+can be isolated; `IMTUBE_GL_YCBCR=swap` flips the 16-bit upload type alone,
+the diagnostic to reach for if a driver ever shows the picture with luma and
+chroma exchanged. `ORC_CODE=backup` in the environment makes GStreamer's ORC
+run its plain C code instead of JIT-compiled AltiVec/SSE, which separates an
+ORC fault from everything else.
+
+Titles in Chinese, Japanese or Korean need a font with those glyphs: ImGui's
+built-in font is Latin only and shows `?` otherwise. At startup ImTube merges
+the first system font it finds from a short list (Arial Unicode, Apple SD
+Gothic Neo, AppleGothic, STHeiti, Osaka, PingFang on macOS; Droid Sans
+Fallback, WenQuanYi or Noto CJK on Linux) and prints `[fonts] CJK fallback:
+<file>`. `IMTUBE_CJK_FONT=<file.ttf>` picks a different one, `IMTUBE_CJK_FONT=none`
+skips it. Glyphs are rasterised on demand, so the cost is the font file's size
+in memory.
 
 ### Linux PC (SDL3 + GLES)
 
